  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178864/assets/fa57e1d088e5384cfd7a08d524877e6a9fafce413f0b3039d59d90dd074d361b.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true for the polygons $P$, $Q$ and $R$ shown above?\n\nI. $P≅Q$\n\nII. $Q≅R$\n\nIII. $P≅R$",
  "worked_solution": "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the polygons $P$ and $Q$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent angles ($P$ does not have right angles, while $Q$ does).\n\n- Statement II is false. By the same reasoning as above, we can see that the polygons $Q$ and $R$ are not congruent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the polygons $P$ and $R$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $P≅R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
